git-scm: Git 2.56.1 for 9front, built with APE

This is a subset of the Git sources: what is needed to build git
and its helper programs with pcc, plus the shell scripts and
templates Git installs.  The tests, documentation, translations,
Perl, Python and Tcl parts are left out.  COPYING has Git's licence.

Build and install:

	cd /sys/src/cmd/git-scm
	mk install

This needs the C99 additions to cc and the APE fixes of the same
tree.  Programs and scripts go in /$objtype/bin/git-scm, templates
in /sys/lib/git-scm/share/git-core/templates, and the system-wide
configuration is read from /sys/lib/git-scm/etc/gitconfig.

/bin/git is already taken by git(1), the native implementation, so
put this one in front of it where you want it:

	bind -b /bin/git-scm /bin

The scripts and some commands (rebase -i, mergetool, filter-branch)
run /bin/ape/sh and expect POSIX tools such as sed and awk in $path.
Output is not paged unless GIT_PAGER or core.pager says so (try p).
Git uses its default editor, ed, only when TERM is set, so set
EDITOR, GIT_EDITOR or core.editor for commit messages.

Not available: http and https transports (no curl), threads, gettext,
iconv, mmap, symbolic links, and the fsmonitor daemon.  The local
and git:// transports work; ssh is untested.

The Plan 9 changes to Git are in plan9/ (headers that complete APE
and a few compat functions) and small portability fixes in Git
proper:
	- function pointer types made to match (odb/source-packed.c,
	  config.c)
	- on-disk headers packed to 4 bytes (pack.h, read-cache-ll.h,
	  pack-revindex.c); 6c pads structures to 8
	- CSPRNG_DEVICE to read /dev/random
	- clone copies objects instead of hard linking them
	- range-diff sizes its memory limit by size_t, which is 32 bits

This directory is generated, mkfile included, from a Git tree that
carries these changes, by plan9/vendor.sh run on a Unix host with
GNU make and Python:

	plan9/vendor.sh /path/to/9front/sys/src/cmd/git-scm

Git's Makefile, with the settings in config.mak (NO_CURL,
NO_PTHREADS, NO_MMAP, NO_REGEX, ...), decides the objects and the
compiler flags for each file.
